  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Portraits and Poses is already showing clear signs of use and visibility. The book has been downloaded 3,409 times through OAPEN, is openly available in hybrid form, and has begun to be cited in scholarly literature. Its topical focus on gender and intellectual authority also aligns with the Sustainable Development Goal on gender equality, and the record shows funding support from several academic and scholarly organizations.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Portraits and Poses addresses a historically grounded question with contemporary relevance: how learned women in Early Modern and Enlightenment Europe negotiated and legitimised intellectual authority through portraiture and other modes of self-representation. That combination of art history, gender history, and the study of learned culture helps explain why the book is surfacing both in scholarly use and in broader knowledge infrastructures.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest usage signal is download activity. With 3,409 OAPEN downloads recorded, the book has reached a substantial audience for a scholarly monograph, suggesting ongoing consultation rather than a purely archival footprint. Its open hybrid availability likely supports that reach by lowering barriers for readers in and beyond the academy.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly uptake is still early but visible. OpenAlex records one citation, appearing in a 2026 reference entry on gender and Reformation in sixteenth-century Germany. Because the citation base is currently small, it is best read as an initial sign of disciplinary resonance rather than as evidence of broad citation impact. The record also shows attention in public knowledge systems, including a Wikipedia mention, which indicates that the book\u2019s subject matter has begun to enter wider reference environments.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book\u2019s thematic alignment with gender equality further reinforces its relevance beyond a single field. Funding from KU Leuven, the Research Foundation Flanders, the Dutch Research Council, and several scholarly foundations points to a project with institutional support across the humanities and historical research landscape. Taken together, the evidence portrays a book with meaningful early reach: well used, openly accessible, and starting to circulate in both scholarly and public-facing knowledge spaces.</p>",
